# Root .gitignore.
# Per-stack build artifacts (target/, proofs/, runs/, extracted images, …) are covered by each
# infra/<stack>-infra/.gitignore — git honors those nested files, so they are not repeated here.

# Upstream clones — external code, tens of GB, not this project's (see README). Holds the witness/guest
# sources (rsp, openvm-eth, zisk-eth-client, zisk-ethproofs) AND the monad client tree, which is a
# working clone on a branch rather than a pinned vendor: cli/install-vendors does not manage it.
/vendor/

# ── guests/ : ignore build outputs + large/regenerable inputs; keep the small data ───────────
# Compiled guest ELFs — rebuild with `cli/gen-elf`. (Monad's ELFs are pre-supplied → kept.)
guests/rsp/*.elf
guests/zisk-reth/*.elf
guests/openvm-reth/*.elf
guests/fibonacci/*.elf
# Monad guest builds that exist ONLY to be measured (compare.py axes): neither guests in the registry
# sense nor pre-supplied — build artifacts like any other, produced on the devcore box. ~68 MB that sat
# in `??`, neither ignored nor committed. What must survive a clone is WHICH BUILD produced which
# number: guests/monad-variants/README.md IS tracked and carries the sha256s, the same contract as a
# generation's PROVENANCE.md.
guests/monad-variants/*/*.elf

# Block witnesses / inputs — large; regenerate with `cli/gen-witness` (or mint on the box).
# KEPT on purpose: `*.commit` pins (small — they pin the ELF + inputs together), NOT matched below.
guests/*/inputs/*.bin
guests/*/inputs/*.hints
guests/*/inputs/*.input.json
guests/*/inputs/*.stdin
guests/*/inputs/rpc-cache/

# fixtures/ is entirely generated and never tracked: the witness staging area (pre-mined witnesses, dropped
# in from a node) and the prover QUEUE that cli/witness-farm / infra/monad-witness/witness-tap fill. Only
# witnesses live there — derived artefacts (exec-reports, public values) belong to a RUN, so they go in the
# run record instead. Ignored wholesale rather than by extension, so a new witness format or a .part
# temporary cannot slip into `git status`.
guests/*/fixtures/

# Execute reports — regenerable locally via `./run execute`. They feed profiling/results.html
# (itself regenerated, not shipped), so they are NOT committed; re-run `./run execute` then results.py.
guests/*/inputs/*.exec-report.json
# RSP writes an execution report to ./report.csv (its default --report-path) when run from the root
# without the flag. No call in this repo does that (infra/sp1-infra/scripts/block-cycles.py always
# passes --report-path), so it is fallout from a manual run: ignored where it lands.
/report.csv

# Monad guest is pre-supplied and NOT regenerable. The whole inputs/ dir (witnesses ~79 MB + their
# post_state_root ground-truth) is kept OUT of git — a fresh clone must copy it in (provenance:
# guests/monad/README.md). Execute outputs + the exec-verified.csv recap regenerate via ev.sh → ignored.
guests/monad/execute-out/
guests/monad/inputs/
guests/monad/exec-verified.csv

# Monad guest artefacts are stored per GENERATION — a guest lineage plus the witness wire format it
# reads, which change together (PartialTrieDb + RLP node list → OffsetTrieDb + offset blob). Bulk is
# regenerable and stays out; the per-generation PROVENANCE.md is small, is the only record of which
# branch and which ELF produced a set of numbers, and IS meant to be committed.
# No trailing slash on `witnesses`: a generation whose guest lineage moved while the wire format did not
# points it at the corpus it shares instead of copying gigabytes, and git treats a symlink as a FILE — the
# same reason `guests/*/fixtures/` cannot cover `fixtures` below. Committed, the link would dangle in every
# clone, since what it points at is ignored. The sharing is recorded in that generation's PROVENANCE.md.
guests/monad/gen/*/witnesses
guests/monad/gen/*/elf/
# `current` selects the generation and `fixtures` is the stable path the tooling globs
# (profiling/compare.py, infra/monad-openvm/segments.sh). Both are local state — a clone has no
# artefacts to point at — and `guests/*/fixtures/` above cannot cover `fixtures` now that it is a
# symlink: a trailing-slash pattern matches directories, and git treats a symlink as a file.
guests/monad/current
guests/monad/fixtures
# (`fixtures-v2` used to have its own rule here: a set dropped BESIDE the layout instead of inside it,
# which compare.py consulted BEFORE `fixtures` and therefore won silently, while use-gen still selected
# the other generation. It became gen/offsettriedb-rework-2026-08 on 2026-08-10. A set landing outside a
# generation is the sign that one is missing — not that a rule is needed.)

# ── profiling/ : all generated output (regenerate: `profiling/results.py`, `profiling/hotspots.py`) ─
profiling/results/
# The profile cache, in its per-block form (profiling/cache-format.md). It used to live under results/
# and was therefore ignored by ricochet; moving out of that directory lost it that cover. A cache is
# regenerable by construction — needing a fact FROM the cache inside a tracked file is the sign that the
# fact is in the wrong place: numbers are re-reported through `migrate-cache.py`, not frozen into docs.
profiling/cache/

# ── run-data/ : ALL runtime output of the cli/ drivers (the drivers are tracked, what they produce is
# not). One rule rather than one per producer: the root no longer gets dirty when a driver gains an
# output, and a new file cannot show up as `??` while waiting for someone to remember to ignore it.
# Every script reaches it through $RUNDATA (overridable → throwaway trial run).
#   witness-farm      → wcache/ · witness-farm.csv · witness-farm-logs/ · witness-farm-parallel-logs/
#   prove-farm        → prove-farm.csv · prove-farm-logs/ · prove-runs/
#   ethproofs / -mock → ethproofs-logs/ · ethproofs-mock-data/
# CAREFUL: prove-farm.csv and prove-farm-logs/ ALSO exist on the devcore box, read by
# infra/monad-witness/{witness-tap,reorg-recover,archive-logs}. Both sides must move together.
/run-data/

# ── Findings notebooks, shared between us and the agents. Deliberately OUT of git: they are working
# notes and traps, not documentation — tracked documentation lives in infra/*/docs/ and in each
# sub-project's README.md. Convention: FINDINGS.md = a sub-project's notebook,
# <TOPIC>-FINDINGS.md = a focused one. Matched at ANY depth, so the next one is covered by default
# instead of waiting to be noticed in `git status`.
# AGENT-NOTES stands apart (cross-cutting memory, not a topic notebook) and is anchored at the root so
# it cannot swallow a same-named file a sub-project might actually want to commit.
/AGENT-NOTES.md
*FINDINGS.md

# ── Python / OS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.DS_Store

# (The monad client tree now lives under vendor/monad — covered by /vendor/ above, so it no longer
# needs a rule of its own. It had one when it sat at the root, and that rule HAD to be anchored:
# written `monad/`, it matched a directory of that name at ANY depth and therefore swallowed
# `guests/monad/` too, hiding the guest's artifacts from `git status`. If a clone ever returns to the
# root, remember the slash.)
